# myosi first-boot credentials (reference)
#
# Sources, highest priority first (first match wins):
#   /run/credentials/@system/   <- ESP, SMBIOS Type 11, qemu fw_cfg
#   /etc/credstore/<name>       plaintext, 0600
#   /etc/credstore.encrypted/<name>   systemd-creds encrypt, TPM2/host-key
#   /run/credstore/
#   /usr/lib/credstore/<name>   the IMAGE DEFAULT baked by myosi
#
# On the ESP prefer the GLOBAL directory, loader/credentials/<name>.cred,
# over the per-UKI /EFI/Linux/<UKI>.efi.extra.d/<name>.cred: the UKI filename
# carries the image version and rotates on every sysupdate, which would orphan
# a per-UKI directory. Ready-to-copy examples live in credentials/ in the
# myosi repo.
#
# systemd-firstboot (ConditionFirstBoot=yes) reads:
#   firstboot.locale / firstboot.locale-messages -> /etc/locale.conf
#   firstboot.keymap            -> /etc/vconsole.conf
#   firstboot.timezone          -> /etc/localtime symlink
#   firstboot.hostname          -> /etc/hostname
#   system.machine_id           -> /etc/machine-id (if unset by initrd)
#   passwd.hashed-password.root -> /etc/shadow root (mkpasswd -m sha512crypt)
#   passwd.plaintext-password.root -> same, plaintext (avoid)
#   passwd.shell.root           -> /etc/passwd root shell
#
# sshd (AuthorizedKeysFile override in 50-myosi.conf) reads:
#   ssh.authorized_keys.root -> /etc/ssh/authorized_keys.d (read at session
#                               setup, no restart needed)
#   ssh.ephemeral-authorized_keys-all -> same, ephemeral
#
# data-luks unlock does NOT use credentials: the initrd
# myosi-data-attach.service probes the baked key file at
# /usr/share/myosi/keys/data.key, then falls through to
# systemd-cryptsetup (TPM2 token, then console passphrase).
#
# ---- Quick-start examples ----
#
#   printf 'myhost-01' \
#     | systemd-creds encrypt --name=firstboot.hostname --with-key=host - \
#         /etc/credstore.encrypted/firstboot.hostname
#
#   systemd-creds encrypt --name=ssh.authorized_keys.root --with-key=tpm2 \
#       ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub /etc/credstore.encrypted/ssh.authorized_keys.root
#
# ---- USB / target-disk delivery ----
# Drop creds at loader/credentials/<name>.cred on the ESP; systemd-stub packs
# them into an initrd cpio and PID 1 exposes them under @system. This works on
# a disk that has NEVER booted, which is the point: /etc lives on data-luks,
# and repart only creates that on first boot. The ESP is the only writable
# surface in between.
#
# Encrypting with --with-key=tpm2 is NOT possible before first boot: the seal
# is host-specific. Pre-boot creds are plaintext on vfat, so ship public keys
# and password hashes there, never secrets.
